nd did you have any fears  being  openly gay entering the genre of country  music  oh for sure i mean you know for me i've  loved country music my whole life  yeah and honestly i thought that once i  um once i realized that i was gay and  was not going to be in the closet about  it  i felt like a performing career was off  the table for me  um but i feel lucky in that i  um it wasn't you know it was sort of  then that things started to happen for  me  and fortunately for me there was no  there was no discussion about  being in the closet because i wasn't so  that was it's never been anything that's  even been talked about  on a record label level at least with me  around  and um and i don't even think with me  not around  um so i was lucky my mom was a huge  katie lang fan oh wow canada  canadian yes yes and i think that there  was a part of me  that always knew even though i was gay  that there would be a place for me in  country music and i think katie lang had  a lot to do with that  you and brandi carlisle are great  friends you've worked together what is  that friendship  meant to you within this industry well  you know i was a fan of brandy's  um a long ways back and the first time  that i met her  was um we did a few shows together  on the road and she's such a great  singer  like as great of a singer as she is on  record like live and so i was a big fan  of brandy's and became a bigger fan  through that and then you know started  to get to know her some  and um when i was in during the pandemic  one of the  great things that happened was i got to  record some songs with her  which probably wouldn't have happened  had we not all been locked down  and that was amazing because i've worked  with really great producers  but she's the first producer i've worked  with that her instrument is her voice  and so she really pushed me in a  different way  the two of you together are really  something special like  honestly yeah well i hope we make a  whole record together  you know i mean i have plans for that i  hope she does too  well you've worked with some cr  incredible people in your time  but i do want to hit on something here  nashville  is full of incredible queer people both  in front and behind the scenes but  nashville  and the country genre still has a long  way to go  with representation that is for sure we  are seeing people like tj osborne come  out love tj love brothers osborne  there's lily rose we're seeing people  like  orville peck and you know even trixie  mattel who was a drag performer getting  a bit of  play within the country genre  do you feel like there's a positive  shift happening i do  i do i mean i've seen things change a  lot in the time i've been in the  business  but i believe in general we  underestimate our audience  i believe the audience is way more  involved  in country music than um then they're  giving credit for  i think you're absolutely right on that  finally brandi i just want to know  what would your advice be to maybe  younger queer  artists who love country music but feel  like there may not be a place for them  within the genre you know i would just  say be great  because you know i remember early on in  knowing  shane macanally who really has been a  pivotal person in my life  for a lot of reasons i think one of the  bigger ones though was when i met him  he was an out of the closet gay man in  nashville which  at the time i didn't really know many  men and i gained a lot of um  my own strength from him and  but i said something one time to him  about um  do you think anybody will like be  against us because we're gay  and he said to me it won't matter we're  too good  yes and i think that that's really the  advice i would give is whatever  whatever it is just be great at  at your craft i believe that i believe  there's always a space  for greatness
 

